#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299582 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.167em}5,\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{11}⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨7,\\hspace{0.167em}-2,\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{11}⟩.$\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.167em}5,\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{11}⟩\\cdot ⟨7,\\hspace{0.167em}-2,\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{11}⟩ \\\\  & =(0)(7)+(5)(-2)+(\\sqrt{11})(\\sqrt{11}) \\\\  & =0-10+11 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299583 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,-1,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨0,4,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨-2,1,5⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using a determinant.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 1)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-1)\\hspace{0.167em}(0\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-2))\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}(19)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-1)\\hspace{0.167em}(2)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(8) \\\\  & =38+2+24 \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299584 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,2,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨4,0,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨-2,-1,5⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using a determinant.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}(0\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-1))\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot 5-1\\cdot (-2))\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}(1)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(22)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(-4) \\\\  & =1-44-12 \\\\  & =-55.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299585 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨0,2,5⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨3,1,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨4,-1,-2⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using a determinant.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}5\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\hspace{0.167em}(1\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot (-1))\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(3\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 4)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}5\\hspace{0.167em}(3\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =0(-2)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(-6)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}5\\hspace{0.167em}(-7) \\\\  & =0+12-35 \\\\  & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299586 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,2,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨4,0,-2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨5,6,1⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using a determinant.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 6)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 5)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot 6-0\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}(12)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(14)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(24) \\\\  & =12-28+72 \\\\  & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299587 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,1,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,4,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨6,-1,2⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using a determinant.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1))\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}1\\hspace{0.167em}(1\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 6)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(1\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}(8)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}1\\hspace{0.167em}(2)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(-25) \\\\  & =16-2-75 \\\\  & =-61.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299588 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,0,2⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨3,1,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨4,2,5⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using a determinant.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}(1\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 2)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}0\\hspace{0.167em}(3\\cdot 5-0\\cdot 4)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}(5)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}0\\hspace{0.167em}(15)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}2\\hspace{0.167em}(2) \\\\  & =5+0+4 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299589 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,1,0⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨0,3,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨5,2,1⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using a determinant.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}(3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}1\\hspace{0.167em}(0\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 5)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}0\\hspace{0.167em}(0\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 5) \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}(-5)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}1\\hspace{0.167em}(-20)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}0\\hspace{0.167em}(-15) \\\\  & =-10+20+0 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299590 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-1,-2,3⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨4,-1,-2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using a determinant.\n\n$\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\hspace{0.167em}((-1)(-3)-(-2)\\cdot 2)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-2)\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot 1)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(4\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(-1)\\hspace{0.167em}(7)\\hspace{0.278em}-\\hspace{0.278em}(-2)\\hspace{0.167em}(-10)\\hspace{0.278em}+\\hspace{0.278em}3\\hspace{0.167em}(9) \\\\  & =-7-20+27 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299591 :question/problem "Calculate $det(R)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(R)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(R) & =\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(8)(4)-(-5)(3) \\\\  & =32+15 \\\\  & =47.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299592 :question/problem "Calculate $det(S)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(S)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2)(3)-(4)(1) \\\\  & =6-4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299593 :question/problem "Calculate $det(S)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(S)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(6)(7)-(-2)(5) \\\\  & =42+10 \\\\  & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299594 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}),$ if $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨4,-1,6⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2,3,-1⟩.$\n\n$\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨2,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,-1,6⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =8-3-6 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) &=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299596 :question/problem "Calculate $det(D)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(D)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{2}{5} & -3 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299597 :question/problem "Calculate $det(E)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle E=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4} & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(E)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(E) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{3}{4} & 5 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (-1)-5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-10 \\\\  & =-\\frac{37}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299598 :question/problem "Calculate $det(D)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}3x & 2y \\\\ -5 & x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n$det(D)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3x & 2y \\\\ -5 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3x\\cdot x-2y\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+10y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299599 :question/problem "Evaluate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & y \\\\ 4 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & y \\\\ 4 & \\sqrt{6}\\end{vmatrix} & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}-y\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6-4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299600 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & m \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(D)=-11,$ determine the value of $m.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & m \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 5-m\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =10-3m.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(D)=-11,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =-11 \\\\ 10-3m & =-11 \\\\ -3m & =-21 \\\\ m & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299601 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}1+r & 2-r \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(U)=-6$, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1+r & 2-r \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(1+r)\\cdot 4-(2-r)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =4+4r+10-5r \\\\  & =14-r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(U)=-6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(U) & =-6 \\\\ 14-r & =-6 \\\\ -r & =-20 \\\\ r & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299602 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}3+s & 2s \\\\ -4 & 3-s\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand $det(S)=25,$ what is the value of $s?$\n\n$s=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3+s & 2s \\\\ -4 & 3-s\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(3+s)(3-s)-2s(-4) \\\\  & =9-{s}^{2}+8s \\\\  & =-{s}^{2}+8s+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(S)=25,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =25 \\\\ -{s}^{2}+8s+9 & =25 \\\\ -{s}^{2}+8s-16 & =0 \\\\ {s}^{2}-8s+16 & =0 \\\\ (s-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ s & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299657 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{a}=⟨5,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,4⟩,$ then $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =10+4 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299658 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{u}=⟨8,-2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨3,6⟩,$ then $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v} & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  & =8\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =24-12 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299660 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 2k \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},$ $\\mathbf{d}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ and $\\mathbf{c}⊥\\mathbf{d},$ then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{c}⊥\\mathbf{d}$ ($\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ are perpendicular) if and only if $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d}=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d} & ={c}_{x}\\cdot {d}_{x}+{c}_{y}\\cdot {d}_{y}+{c}_{z}\\cdot {d}_{z} \\\\  & =k\\cdot (-3)+2k\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-3k+4k+8 \\\\  & =k+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot \\mathbf{d} & =0 \\\\ k+8 & =0 \\\\ k & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299664 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2{x}^{2},-4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨8,4⟩.$ If $\\mathbf{a}⊥\\mathbf{b}$ and $x>0,$ then $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{a}⊥\\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular) if and only if $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}\\cdot 8+(-4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16{x}^{2}-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-16 & =0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are told that $x>0.$ Therefore, $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299701 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\hspace{0.167em}|=2\\sqrt{2}$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{l}$ is $\\theta ={120}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{k}$ onto $\\mathbf{l}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{l}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{l}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299702 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r}\\hspace{0.167em}|=10$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{r}$ and $\\mathbf{s}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{r}$ onto $\\mathbf{s}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{s}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{s}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =10cos\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299703 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|=8$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{p}$ and $\\mathbf{q}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =8cos\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299704 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{t}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{2}$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{t}$ and $\\mathbf{u}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{t}$ onto $\\mathbf{u}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{u}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{t}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{u}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{t} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{t}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}cos\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299705 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r}\\hspace{0.167em}|=3\\sqrt{3}$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{r}$ and $\\mathbf{s}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{r}$ onto $\\mathbf{s}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{s}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{s}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}cos\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299706 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨4,-1,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨1,2,2⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4)(1)+(-1)(2)+(1)(2)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-2+2}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299707 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨3,-2,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,1,-2⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)(2)+(-2)(1)+(1)(-2)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-2-2}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299708 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{r}=⟨-1,2,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{s}=⟨2,0,0⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{r}$ onto $\\mathbf{s}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{s}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{s}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{r} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{s}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{s}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{r}_{x}{s}_{x}+{r}_{y}{s}_{y}+{r}_{z}{s}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{s}_{x}^{2}+{s}_{y}^{2}+{s}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)(2)+(2)(0)+(3)(0)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299709 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{m}=⟨6,8,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{n}=⟨0,5,12⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{m}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{n}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{m}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{m}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{m}_{x}{n}_{x}+{m}_{y}{n}_{y}+{m}_{z}{n}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{n}_{x}^{2}+{n}_{y}^{2}+{n}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6)(0)+(8)(5)+(0)(12)}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{12}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{\\sqrt{169}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{m}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{m}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{m}_{x}{n}_{x}+{m}_{y}{n}_{y}+{m}_{z}{n}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{m}_{x}^{2}+{m}_{y}^{2}+{m}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{8}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{n}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{m}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{m}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{40}{13}-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{40-52}{13} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299710 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨9,12,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨0,3,4⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{p}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{p}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(9)(0)+(12)(3)+(0)(4)}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{p}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}}{|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}\\hspace{0.167em}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}+{p}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{{9}^{2}+{12}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\sqrt{225}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{q}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{p}-{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{p}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{q} & =\\frac{36}{5}-\\frac{12}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299726 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨3,-1,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨x,1,1⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 3x+1 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299731 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨4,1,-2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨1,x,3⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+1\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299732 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-2,3,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨1,2,x⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =-2+6+4x \\\\  & =4x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 4x+4 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299736 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=2\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{q}=x\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =2\\cdot x+(-3)\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 2x-12 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =12 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299737 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=-4\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{q}=5\\mathbf{i}+x\\mathbf{j}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 5+2\\cdot x+(-3)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-20+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ -20+2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =20 \\\\ x & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299738 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{q}=-2\\mathbf{i}+x\\mathbf{j}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}$ is zero, find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-6+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ -6+2x & =0 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
